.TH PAM-DEVICE-AUTH 8 "2026-11-13" "0.7.1" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
pam-device-auth \- two-factor SSH login (directory key + OIDC device grant)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pam-device-auth
[\fB\-\-check\fR]
[\fB\-\-setup\-ldap\fR]
[\fB\-\-enable\fR]
[\fB\-\-migrate\-config\fR]
[\fB\-\-prepare\-log\fR]
[\fB\-\-debug\fR]
[\fB\-\-version\fR]
[\fB\-\-help\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pam-device-auth
provides two-factor SSH login: an SSH key served from an LDAP directory
.RB ( publickey )
plus the OIDC Device Authorization Grant (RFC 8628). Identity, groups, home
directory and the sudo password come from the directory via SSSD/NSS.
pam-device-auth is the OIDC layer: it validates an access token (signature,
issuer, client binding, expiry, required role, optional authentication level)
for the SSH user and grants or denies the login. It never reads
.IR /etc/shadow ,
prompts for a local password, or creates users.
.PP
For the OIDC factor the user sees a URL and code in their terminal, authorizes
in a browser, and the session is granted. The Go binary is invoked by the C PAM
module
.RB ( pam_device_auth.so )
during SSH keyboard-interactive authentication, and by the account phase for
the IP allowlist check (see below). It is not typically run directly by
users, except for the
.BR \-\-check ,
.B \-\-setup\-ldap
and
.B \-\-enable
commands. The
.B \-\-pam\-acct
mode is invoked internally by the PAM stack and is not meant for interactive use.
.PP
Two directory group tiers control SSH access: members of
.I access_group
log in with both factors (key + OIDC); members of
.I service_account_groups
are service/automation accounts that log in key-only, with no OIDC step.
Sudo is a separate axis:
.I admin_group
gets password sudo via pam_sss, and
.I nopasswd_sudo_groups
gets passwordless sudo. A user needs membership in at least one of the
access-tier groups to log in at all.
.PP
Every login, including key-only service accounts, is checked against a
per-user source-IP/CIDR allowlist read from the directory
.I clients
attribute, enforced in the PAM account phase over the SSSD InfoPipe
(busctl). Only root is exempt from this check (break-glass); it never touches
SSSD for root. Local non-root users still go through the SSSD InfoPipe lookup.
The check fails closed: if the
.I clients
lookup for a directory user or a local non-root user fails, that login is denied, so an InfoPipe/SSSD outage denies all
directory users, not just the ones with an allowlist set.
.PP
Works on OpenSSH 10.2+, which refuses PAM for users not resolvable via
.BR getpwnam (3).
Directory users resolve through NSS, so they always exist.
.PP
.BR \-\-check ,
.B \-\-setup\-ldap
and
.B \-\-enable
first check the platform (see REQUIREMENTS) and refuse every other
distribution before they read the config or change the host:
.PP
.RS
.nf
unsupported distribution "<ID> <VERSION_ID>" (supported: ubuntu >= 24.04,
debian >= 13, rocky 9/10, fedora >= 44)
.fi
.RE
.PP
All three refuse an
.I ldap
block without
.I profile
(a [FAIL] item in
.BR \-\-check ):
.PP
.RS
.nf
config: ldap.profile is required ("lldap" or "ad")
.fi
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-check
Run the pre-activation preflight: validate the config, test OIDC discovery and
issuer match, verify the directory path (SSSD running, access group resolves via
NSS, every access-group member has a valid
.IR sshPublicKey ),
the sshd factors (AuthorizedKeysCommand, UsePAM and
KbdInteractiveAuthentication, non-root 2FA, the methods sshd applies to each
member of the access group and the service groups, root break-glass), IP
allowlist posture, and SSSD InfoPipe health. It reports how long OIDC
discovery took and warns when two requests in a row took more than 2 seconds.
The member lists are what NSS reports. With profile
.B ad
an account that has the access group or a service group as its primary group
is listed only once the host has resolved it; the checks per member can leave
it out, and an [INFO] line says so.
Config keys the loader does not know
and
.I allowed_algorithms
entries the verifier does not support are [FAIL] items here, in
.B \-\-enable
and in
.BR \-\-setup\-ldap ;
a login logs them as warnings and goes on. It also verifies that
.I sssd.conf
exposes the
.I clients
attribute to the InfoPipe
.RB ( "ldap_user_extra_attrs = clients:<attribute>"
and
.BR "[ifp] user_attributes = +clients" ),
that
.I ldap.uri
is a single
.B ldaps://
or
.B ldap://
(StartTLS) URI and which transport it gives, that
.I ldap.profile
is set, that the login shell
.RI ( ldap.shell ,
.I /bin/bash
when unset) is executable, that
.I ldap.ca_file
and the Kerberos keytab are in place, that the UIDs and GIDs of directory
users exist in the host's ID map, root's sshd policy (including its
AuthorizedKeysFile) for every
.I root_login_sources
address, and that the AuthorizedKeysCommand binary and, when
.I sssd.conf
enables it, the InfoPipe responder exist at the platform's path. Local accounts that
shadow a directory user are listed as warnings with what
.B \-\-setup\-ldap
will do (migrate or skip).
Each item is reported as [OK]/[WARN]/[FAIL]; exits non-zero if any blocker is found.
.TP
.B \-\-setup\-ldap
Configure directory identity from the
.I ldap
block of the config: write
.IR /etc/sssd/sssd.conf
(including the InfoPipe
.I ifp
responder needed for the IP allowlist check), add
.B sss
to nsswitch, enable
.BR pam_mkhomedir ,
install the sudoers rules for the admin and NOPASSWD sudo groups, write the
sshd drop-in, and migrate any pre-existing local accounts that shadow a
directory user (no process is killed; an account with running processes is
skipped with a warning). The required
.I ldap.profile
selects LLDAP
.RB ( lldap )
or Active Directory
.RB ( ad ).
Uses the platform's tools and paths (apt/dnf, pam-auth-update/authselect, CA
path,
.BR sss_ssh_authorizedkeys
path); it also installs the
.B sssd-dbus
package needed for the InfoPipe responder and, with
.IR ldap.kerberos ,
the SSSD Kerberos provider and the SASL GSSAPI plugin. Before the first change
to the host it checks the config, the login shell of directory users
.RI ( ldap.shell ,
.I /bin/bash
when unset), the package index refresh
.RB ( "apt-get update"
on Debian and Ubuntu), the package install and marking the packages as
manually installed, and that
.B sss_ssh_authorizedkeys
and the InfoPipe responder are present. On Rocky Linux and Fedora it also runs
.B authselect check
and stops when that names a file changed outside of authselect, other than
this command's own edit of
.IR /etc/nsswitch.conf :
the
.B authselect select
of the setup would overwrite it. Every later step is checked too. A
failed step aborts with one
.B FAIL:
line naming the command and its output, what the rollback did (the previous
.I sssd.conf
restored or the new one removed, and sssd restarted on it once it was running
on the new file), and the changes still in place on the host. A failed
local-account migration keeps the new
.IR sssd.conf .
Does not restart sshd. Idempotent. Requires root.
.TP
.B \-\-enable
Activate PAM authentication. Backs up
.IR /etc/pam.d/sshd ,
installs the PAM config, copies the sshd PAM template, and restarts sshd
.RB ( ssh.service
on Debian and Ubuntu,
.B sshd.service
on Rocky Linux and Fedora). Runs the
.B \-\-check
preflight first and refuses to activate on a blocker. The backup to
.I /etc/pam.d/sshd.original
comes before any write: when it fails, or when no pristine PAM config is
left to save (a missing
.IR /etc/pam.d/sshd ,
or one that uses
.B pam_device_auth.so
while no valid backup exists), it exits 1 without changing the host. Requires
root.
.TP
.B \-\-migrate\-config
Set
.B \(dqprofile\(dq: \(dqlldap\(dq
in the
.I ldap
block of
.I /etc/pam-device-auth/config.json
when it has no
.I profile
(or
.B null
or
.BR \(dq\(dq ).
The original is kept as
.I config.json.pre\-0.7.0
with the same owner and mode; the new file is written atomically with the
original's owner and mode, with sorted keys, and checked with the loader of
.BR \-\-check .
A config without an
.I ldap
block, or with
.B lldap
or
.BR ad ,
is not touched. Any other profile, duplicate keys that the loader would read
differently after the rewrite, a symlink, an existing backup or unreadable
JSON is an error that leaves the file as it is (exit 1). Run by the package's
post-install script on every install or upgrade that finds a config.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Enable verbose logging to
.IR /var/log/pam-device-auth.log .
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-prepare\-log
Create
.I /var/log/pam-device-auth.log
or check the existing one: it must be a regular file (a symlink, a FIFO or a
directory at the path is an error), mode bits beyond 0640 are removed, and the
file is given to root and the group adm where that group exists. The package
runs it on install and upgrade.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.PP
An additional
.B \-\-pam\-acct
mode exists for the PAM account phase to enforce the IP allowlist; it is
invoked internally by the PAM stack and not meant to be run by hand. It exits
0 (allow), 1 (deny for this user) or 3 when it cannot open its log, the log
path is no regular file, or the log has mode bits beyond 0640 that cannot be
removed: then it denies every non-root user and reports the reason to syslog
.RB ( authpriv ).
The PAM module logs exit 3 as an infrastructure failure and denies the login.
A log with mode 0640 or stricter is not changed.
.PP
The authentication phase denies the login in the same case and reports it to
syslog
.RB ( authpriv ,
tag
.BR pam-device-auth ),
for a 0644 log made append-only with
.B "chattr +a"
for example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
pam-device-auth: cannot set log file mode 0640: chmod
/var/log/pam-device-auth.log: operation not permitted; denying login
(fail closed)
.fi
.RE
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration file:
.IR /etc/pam-device-auth/config.json .
.PP
.TS
l l l l.
Field	Required	Default	Description
_
issuer_url	Yes	\-	OIDC issuer URL
client_id	Yes	\-	OAuth2 client ID (public)
required_role	Yes	\-	Role claim value for SSH access
sudo_role	No	\-	Directory group/role for sudo
role_claim	No	\-	Extra JWT claim path for roles
auth_timeout	No	180	Device flow timeout (30\-240s)
allowed_algorithms	No	\-	Pin accepted JWT alg (e.g. ["RS256"])
show_qr	No	auto	QR display: true/false/auto-detect
root_login	No	key	key (root SSH key) or disabled
required_acr	No	\-	Accepted acr claim values (exact match)
root_login_sources	No	\-	Client IPs for root's policy in \-\-check
ldap	setup	\-	Directory block for \-\-setup\-ldap (see below)
.TE
.PP
sshd's
.B LoginGraceTime
(default 120 seconds, counted from the moment the connection is opened) bounds
the whole login, including the wait for the approval, and is shorter than the
default auth_timeout. To use a longer wait, set
.B LoginGraceTime
in a file in
.I /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/
that sorts before
.IR 10-pam-device-auth.conf .
.PP
The
.I ldap
block (used by
.B \-\-setup\-ldap
and
.BR \-\-check )
holds
.I profile
.RB ( lldap
or
.BR ad ,
required),
.IR uri ", " base_dn ", " bind_dn ", " bind_password
(or
.IR bind_password_file ,
or neither with a Kerberos keytab),
.I access_group
and
.IR admin_group ,
plus two opt-in lists:
.I service_account_groups
(directory groups whose members log in key-only, with no OIDC step) and
.I nopasswd_sudo_groups
(groups granted passwordless sudo). Optional keys:
.IR ca_file ", " attributes ", " id_mapping ", " id_range ,
.IR home ", " shell ", " kerberos ", " user_search_base
and
.IR group_search_base .
There is no cleartext transport:
.B ldap://
always means StartTLS, or GSSAPI with SASL sealing for a keytab bind. The
read-only bind password is written into the root-only
.I /etc/sssd/sssd.conf
for SSSD's runtime use and is not read by the auth runtime.
.PP
Per-user source-IP allowlisting uses the directory
.I clients
attribute (set on the directory user entry, not in config.json; the attribute
name comes from
.IR ldap.attributes.clients )
and is enforced for every login by the PAM account phase.
.PP
The fields issuer_url, client_id, required_role, sudo_role, role_claim and
auth_timeout can be overridden via environment variables prefixed with
.B PAM_DEVICE_AUTH_
for a command run by hand. They do not reach a login that sshd starts: sshd
runs the PAM conversation with an empty environment. The variables are
deprecated; 0.8.0 removes them.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/pam-device-auth/config.json
OIDC provider and directory configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/pam-device-auth/config.json.pre\-0.7.0
Copy of the config from before
.B \-\-migrate\-config
set
.IR ldap.profile .
.TP
.I /usr/lib/security/pam_device_auth.so
C PAM shared library (deb; rpm installs to
.IR /usr/lib64/security ).
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/pam-device-auth
Go binary.
.TP
.I /run/pam-device-auth/
Token cache directory (tmpfs, cleared on reboot). Mode 0700 root:root.
.TP
.I /var/log/pam-device-auth.log
Authentication log file.
.TP
.I /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/10-pam-device-auth.conf
sshd configuration (KbdInteractiveAuthentication yes, LogLevel VERBOSE;
LogVerbose for the client version line).
.TP
.I /etc/sssd/sssd.conf
Directory wiring written by \-\-setup\-ldap (0600 root), including the
InfoPipe responder used for the IP allowlist check. SSSD reads it at runtime.
.TP
.I /etc/sudoers.d/pam-device-auth
sudo rules for the admin and NOPASSWD sudo groups, written by
\-\-setup\-ldap and removed on uninstall.
.SH AUTHENTICATION FLOW
.PP
Non-root logins in
.I access_group
require both factors:
.B publickey
(the user's key, served from the directory by
.BR sss_ssh_authorizedkeys )
.B and
.B keyboard-interactive
(the OIDC device grant). Members of
.I service_account_groups
log in with the key alone; every login, key-only or not, is still subject
to the
.I clients
IP allowlist check in the PAM account phase.
.PP
.B First login / new IP:
full OIDC Device Authorization Grant. User sees URL + code (+ QR code if the
client supports UTF-8), authorizes in a browser. The token is validated
(required role and, if set, the accepted
.I acr
values) and the login is granted.
.PP
.B Known IP:
silent OIDC token refresh, re-validated, no browser round-trip while the
session at the identity provider lives. The SSH key factor is always required.
.PP
.B Root:
SSH key only
.RB ( "AuthenticationMethods publickey" )
against root's local authorized_keys, and exempt from the
.I clients
IP allowlist check. Root key-only login is the break-glass path, independent
of OIDC/SSSD/LDAP availability. Other local users cannot log in: sshd reads no
local authorized_keys for them, the OIDC step requires a directory identity,
and the account phase fails closed for a user the SSSD InfoPipe does not
know. If
.I root_login
is set to
.B disabled
in the configuration, the sshd drop-in sets
.B PermitRootLogin no
and root cannot log in over SSH at all.
.SH SECURITY
.PP
Two factors (directory SSH key + OIDC), with full token validation on every
login: signature, issuer, azp/aud, exp/nbf/iat, required role and the optional
.I required_acr
level; JWT algorithm/key-type binding, kid required, no none/HMAC. The per-user
.I clients
IP allowlist is enforced separately in the PAM account phase and fails
closed on a directory/InfoPipe lookup error. Directory
revocation: removing a user from the access group denies new logins and sudo
within the SSSD cache TTL. sudo re-authenticates against the directory password
via pam_sss, except for members of
.IR nopasswd_sudo_groups .
The C PAM shim uses fork/exec (no shell), clearenv with an env
whitelist, pipe2(O_CLOEXEC), a per-call timeout, SIGPIPE handling and log
sanitization. The refresh-token cache lives in tmpfs (0700 root:root, cleared
on reboot) with the KnownIPs list capped (FIFO).
.PP
See
.I SECURITY.md
in the source repository for the full threat model.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.PP
amd64 and one of: Ubuntu 24.04 or later, Debian 13 or later, Rocky Linux 9 or
10, Fedora 44 or later (the
.B ID
and
.B VERSION_ID
of
.IR /etc/os-release ).
Every other distribution is refused by the package pre-install script and by
.BR \-\-check ,
.B \-\-setup\-ldap
and
.BR \-\-enable .
OpenSSH >= 9.6p1.
.PP
Removing the package is refused while
.I /etc/pam.d/sshd
uses
.B pam_device_auth.so
and no valid
.I /etc/pam.d/sshd.original
exists; restore
.I /etc/pam.d/sshd
from the distribution's openssh-server package first. A failing
.B "sshd \-t"
or sshd restart fails the package script that runs it.
SSH client auto-detection (QR code display) needs the
.B LogVerbose
line of the managed sshd drop-in, which journals the client version.
